#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161754 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/id #uuid "2ac70ed6-4f03-59ae-9a3b-b8a93386507d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8021" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c5e52c78f925dc4e01b1098b507865407f9848cc1db6e8a946ad81840e8e4ee.png)\n\n  \n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X \\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(2) = 4$\n\n- $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y$\n\n- The function $f:X \\to Y$ is **into**" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed,   there is an arrow from $2 \\in X$ to $4\\in Y.$ So,  $f(2)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $1 \\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $1 \\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=1$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x) \\neq y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161755 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "346ff48a-b54f-56cc-b5b2-4437c7d3d94d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8022" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-1)^2-2?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[-2, \\infty)$\n\n- $\\forall\\, y \\in [-2, \\infty),$ $\\exists\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\n- $f$ maps $\\mathbb{R}$ **into** $\\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/35949b4a4809821871a9edf9466fa00862778094d754674014407525b5fc2fed.png)\n\nRecall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-2,$ and it occurs at the point $(1,-2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values higher than $y=-2$, including $y=-2$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $[-2, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $[-2, \\infty)$, then for all $y$ contained within $[-2, \\infty),$ there exists some $x$ contained within $\\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x) = y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=-3 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ So, $f$ maps $\\mathbb{R}$ *into* $\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II,  and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161756 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "d3e8ba8d-5a83-5683-9e19-33f021459ceb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8023" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n- $f: [-2, \\infty) \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x) =\\sqrt{ x+2}$\n\n- $g: \\mathbb{R} \\to [0, \\infty)$ defined by $g(x) = |x-2|+3$\n\n- $h:(0,\\infty) \\to\\Bbb R$ defined by $h(x) = \\ln(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is an into function. Let's take $-1 \\in\\Bbb R.$ There is no number $x \\in[-2, \\infty)$ such that $f(x) =\\sqrt{ x+2}=-1.$ In fact, $f(x) =\\sqrt{ x+2}  \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in[-2, \\infty) .$\n\n- II is an into function. Let's take $1 \\in [0, \\infty).$ There is no number $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$  such that $g(x)=|x-2|+3=1.$ In fact, $|x-2|+3 \\geq 3$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- III is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y \\in \\Bbb R,$ we can choose $x =e^{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Then $h(x) = h \\left( e^{y}\\right) =\\ln(e^{y}) = y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05cadc09-da0d-5ae7-aeaf-a7c3e35f4b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409928} {:answer/id #uuid "293c9758-a266-51af-a90b-8e6051ffbc3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409944} {:answer/id #uuid "7a906543-e72c-59fe-99da-f316e3cbe719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409960} {:answer/id #uuid "9431a3dc-a0a4-59ec-8755-32a07ab39b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409966} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba1dd0d-6081-556d-8127-a0c2c1fbb715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "293c9758-a266-51af-a90b-8e6051ffbc3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409944} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d1be59e-d97b-50a5-a2c4-37e42fdd9c30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1346b272-ab34-50a3-8e2c-0cbfe3ad7ce0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105231" :question/problem "![](images/37/373448202bfd022f5629219b2c176f4096e4eeae275c1095798d77be92122cc2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(1)=4$\n- $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n- The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There is an arrow from $1\\in X$ to $2\\in Y.$ So, $f(1)=2\\ne 4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $4\\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $4\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=4$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dd34768-3b56-5dbb-8360-edafef0e9154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409951} {:answer/id #uuid "5e866bf5-e3a4-50a8-88cd-3161502266e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409952} {:answer/id #uuid "9af2c882-4937-5a56-810e-fd366ee4d9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409970} {:answer/id #uuid "a464652c-d346-56a1-aae2-90391c124d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409975} {:answer/id #uuid "ee78d345-4ef0-58f9-955e-27910f8ef035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee78d345-4ef0-58f9-955e-27910f8ef035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409990} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d437d45-378d-58da-b396-7f6276280de3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10b365a7-4952-5fcd-929e-00199380cb55" :question/math-academy-id "q-105240" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3c5df109b00450c20acdbc07378bfe309cf808c1244bedd6ff4fa3a850ccd13.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(10)=25$\n- $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n- The function $f:X\\to Y$ is not **into**" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed,   there is an arrow from $10\\in X$ to $25\\in Y.$ So,  $f(10)=25.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, $f$ is *not* an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2197db5e-ca7c-51e6-9937-ef34b85e9ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409940} {:answer/id #uuid "78b5fb43-b40d-5220-9328-04422aadc56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409958} {:answer/id #uuid "a770c062-0f3b-5c66-b3cb-db67c9f4a392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409977} {:answer/id #uuid "af41f11c-e537-562e-a402-a550f60bf7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409981} {:answer/id #uuid "d13b3972-fd08-50cd-9235-d2b960271157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78b5fb43-b40d-5220-9328-04422aadc56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409958} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f95ef80-066f-5862-85e2-305f98933069" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e45af0c1-a25f-5254-88c0-adcdabddd288" :question/math-academy-id "q-105246" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae980a05fc0d25d2696185c88fcf90ff156af9c3f42af7cece80f8c33692a3d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(1)=7$\n- $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n- The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There is no arrow from $1\\in X$ to $7\\in Y.$ So, $f(1)\\ne 7.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $5\\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $5\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=5$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee280af-f80a-5d65-a83c-f42a485b1769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409933} {:answer/id #uuid "68a33e1c-563b-5afc-b17f-ce5f5348dc03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409955} {:answer/id #uuid "97ad711b-b0d6-57f1-b652-f7af62d44cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409968} {:answer/id #uuid "9d93993b-037d-5b0b-9adb-08cfead1e626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409973} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa6a430-3aaf-57ed-b195-d170259f40e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa6a430-3aaf-57ed-b195-d170259f40e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409986} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ef56aef-eb30-5217-a5bd-4df99c5c1ffa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39b57e8a-5e0f-5758-8d6b-0d9667e7e35b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105258" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={2}^{-x}?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[0,\\infty )$\n- $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/c7/c79c1123036c978c8c4da514651c0bb1c8e3df34c8bbaff099bee601d568ed10.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph will cover all $y$-values greater than $y=0$, excluding $y=0$ itself. So the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Take, for example, $y=-1.$ Since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ),$ there can't be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the function is not a surjection.  So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230a40a8-f500-5f2f-a709-31d71dbc148c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409941} {:answer/id #uuid "24207a50-894d-5448-94b0-920d3599e670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409943} {:answer/id #uuid "87b5a6e9-7f66-50a5-a1c5-4245ae5ae0b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409964} {:answer/id #uuid "94b4368d-14b9-539d-b55c-e8ad24e70c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409967} {:answer/id #uuid "e18b5536-8d8b-5756-98f0-ccf4338f0e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24207a50-894d-5448-94b0-920d3599e670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409943} :answer-field/id #uuid "575d2b88-29b9-5fe3-81fe-8497081bd470" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b62af08-9109-5599-b006-f2ceb899c9fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-105263" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to (-\\infty ,1)$ which is given by $f(x)=1-{2}^{x}?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,1)$\n- $∀\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,1],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a function of $R$ **into** $(-\\infty ,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/35bd21eef06d1ea63aba6e420d9842de61f9248c4fa26c2895f6825f4e7a3848.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values lower than $y=1$, excluding $y=1$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,1)$,  there can't be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $1\\notin (-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement I is true, the function is a surjection.  So, $f$ is *not* a function of $R$ *into* $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07dec380-033c-5dbf-a5f5-d2f352afa594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409929} {:answer/id #uuid "0c43997e-0ae2-54ee-8b77-f5d270f7bf43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409931} {:answer/id #uuid "14fbca50-5fc0-5de1-bc35-ddb30ff5e841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409934} {:answer/id #uuid "627656ec-9209-5331-a051-888a272b3c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409954} {:answer/id #uuid "a87ef1c2-9db0-5b5a-b56b-85f769bcbb66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c43997e-0ae2-54ee-8b77-f5d270f7bf43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409931} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f86ac6a-bf48-5e85-a3f9-5713ae66752a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93bb691b-3476-50ac-a75b-0f3bd8e04d2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-90713" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n- $f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$\n- $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{4}$\n- $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={5}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={log}_{2}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{2}⁡y)={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- II is an into function. Let's take $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only numbers $x$ that satisfy $g(x)={x}^{4}=2$ are $x=\\pm \\sqrt[4]{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={5}^{x}=-1.$ In fact, ${5}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e10e89a-3526-5277-97f0-adb1bf3a9d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409932} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8f07e8-6563-560f-83b1-e540eea67b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409938} {:answer/id #uuid "2d675192-6aed-5fb3-b304-c827908d6782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409945} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5d8409-cc3d-50e6-81af-f2299d82d242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409979} {:answer/id #uuid "fae3b726-3cc7-5b0b-9698-60d33fec930f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fae3b726-3cc7-5b0b-9698-60d33fec930f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409991} :answer-field/id #uuid "86ac4b53-a5c2-53ff-9394-b86ac9fdb3ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae359058-a9c8-58ca-9940-1c14f3b8e228" :question/math-academy-id "q-90789" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f68e70397b982a70b372b2bd9e4f73da3374d3abfb2ef4bbd2ec1fffe6dbb31.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(2)=4$\n- $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n- The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   there is an arrow from $2\\in X$ to $4\\in Y.$ So,  $f(2)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is *not* an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179020df-9cb4-5bfd-b5d3-c46b461692c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409937} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9d0d51-0d80-5463-9667-8ab96e0500d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409946} {:answer/id #uuid "7284a027-a46c-51c1-b523-0dd744da21f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409957} {:answer/id #uuid "adc24338-1549-5879-8c00-138c6449cd32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409980} {:answer/id #uuid "ec35298f-a284-5478-97e5-643be853c7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "179020df-9cb4-5bfd-b5d3-c46b461692c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409937} :answer-field/id #uuid "d92eaa22-5617-5adb-9aa6-252e76f8012f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a60aead-59e2-5a19-8c88-b84da12bb8f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-90823" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=2-{x}^{2}?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,-2]$\n- $∀\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,2],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/3575afe4fd4e5aa6754496154986b272a43fb5c035ec8d69621d52fad16ee1ea.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $2,$ and it occurs at the point $(0,2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values lower than $y=2$, including $y=2$ itself. So the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,2]$, then for all $y$ contained within $(-\\infty ,2],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=3\\in R.$ So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fed5044-9dc6-5bdb-b912-3e1c49523233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409939} {:answer/id #uuid "233d2adf-428c-5279-b1bb-5fdd3b009f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 175